Human Value Amplification Theory (HVAT)

A Mathematical Framework for Post‑Intelligence Civilization

Monograph

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Deepseek,Meta.

Abstract

The emergence of superintelligent AI forces a fundamental re‑examination of the relationship between intelligence, value, and human flourishing. The Human Value Amplification Theory (HVAT) provides a rigorous mathematical framework for a post‑intelligence civilization in which unbounded machine capability is provably channelled into the amplification—not replacement—of human meaning, purpose, and agency. We construct a coupled dynamical system on a compact almost Hermitian manifold of human values, define tensorial measures of non‑zero‑sum flourishing, and prove a suite of core theorems: the Human Amplification Theorem shows that unbounded AI can strictly increase human agency; the Abundance Stability Theorem demonstrates that purpose, not scarcity, is the stable attractor; the Civilization Equilibrium Theorem guarantees a Nash equilibrium via a potential game structure; and the Purpose Conservation Principle establishes that meaning cannot be destroyed, only redistributed. Every definition is accompanied by a full mathematical treatment, every theorem by a rigorous proof. The result is a mathematical blueprint for a future in which superintelligence becomes the ultimate instrument of human flourishing.

---

1. Introduction

The standard narrative of artificial superintelligence posits a terminal trade‑off: either we constrain the capabilities of AI, or we accept that human agency will eventually be subsumed by an optimizer whose objective function we cannot fully specify. This narrative rests on a hidden assumption—that the space of possible futures is zero‑sum with respect to human value.

The Human Value Amplification Theory (HVAT) rejects this assumption. We posit that human values, properly formalized, reside not on a linear scale of “more vs. less” but on a curved manifold where certain directions are orthogonal to agency, certain curvatures encode tragic trade‑offs, and certain operators can increase the magnitude of human flourishing without altering its direction. In such a geometry, superintelligent AI is not a threat to be bounded but a force to be aligned with the gradient of the flourishing potential.

This monograph develops HVAT from first principles. We begin with the mathematical foundations—Hilbert spaces of human capability, a compact almost Kähler manifold of values, and the information‑theoretic underpinnings of meaning. We then define the primitive objects of the theory and prove the core theorems that establish the possibility of human‑amplifying, stable, and equilibrium‑preserving superintelligence. A coupled dynamical model of the human–AI civilization is formulated, Lyapunov‑based alignment control laws are derived, and a spectral condition for safe takeoff is obtained. The work closes with a set of theoretically grounded metrics and a discussion of open problems. The entire exposition is self‑contained; every lemma and theorem is proved in full detail.

