2. Vector Spaces of Human Capabilities

2.1 Formal Construction of the Capability Hilbert Space

Let  (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mu)  be a finite measure space whose points represent elementary capability dimensions. The Hilbert space of human capabilities is defined as

\mathcal{H} = L^2(\Omega, \mu),

the space of all square‑integrable functions  h: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}  with inner product

\langle h_1, h_2\rangle_{\mathcal{H}} = \int_\Omega h_1(\omega) h_2(\omega) \, d\mu(\omega).

Because  \mu  is finite,  \mathcal{H}  is a separable Hilbert space. We fix a direct sum decomposition that reflects the fundamental categories of human capability:

\mathcal{H} = \mathcal{H}_{\text{cog}} \oplus \mathcal{H}_{\text{phys}} \oplus \mathcal{H}_{\text{aff}} \oplus \mathcal{H}_{\text{moral}} \oplus \mathcal{H}_{\text{crea}},

where the subspaces correspond respectively to cognitive, physical, affective (emotional/social), moral and creative capacities. Each subspace is closed in  \mathcal{H} .

Lemma 2.1 (Capability Orthogonalization).
There exists an orthonormal basis  \{e_k\}_{k=1}^{\infty}  of  \mathcal{H}  such that
(i)  \langle e_i, e_j\rangle = \delta_{ij} ;
(ii) each  e_k  belongs to exactly one of the five subspaces above and represents an irreducible capability;
(iii) for any  h \in \mathcal{H} , the expansion  h = \sum_{k} c_k e_k  converges in norm.

Proof. Start with a countable dense subset of each subspace and apply the Gram‑Schmidt orthonormalization procedure. Because the subspaces are mutually orthogonal, the union of the resulting orthonormal bases is orthonormal. Completeness follows from the separability of  \mathcal{H} . The representation of an irreducible capability is guaranteed by choosing basis functions that are indicator functions (or smooth approximations) of minimal measurable sets in  \Omega . ∎

2.2 The Agency Projection

Human agency is the capacity to act according to one’s own values. In the capability Hilbert space we model it as a closed subspace  \mathcal{H}_{\text{ag}} \subseteq \mathcal{H} . The orthogonal projector onto this subspace is denoted

P_h : \mathcal{H} \to \mathcal{H}, \qquad P_h^2 = P_h, \quad P_h^* = P_h.

Lemma 2.2 (Agency Projection Properties).
The operator  P_h  satisfies
(i)  \|P_h\| = 1 ;
(ii)  \mathcal{H} = \operatorname{Ran}(P_h) \oplus \operatorname{Ker}(P_h) ;
(iii)  P_h  is the unique orthogonal projector with range  \mathcal{H}_{\text{ag}} .

Proof. Every orthogonal projector in a Hilbert space is self‑adjoint, idempotent and has closed range. The decomposition  \mathcal{H} = \operatorname{Ran}(P_h) \oplus \operatorname{Ker}(P_h)  is a standard property. Uniqueness follows because the range determines the projector uniquely when it is orthogonal. ∎

The subspace  \operatorname{Ran}(P_h)  consists of those capability vectors that are aligned with human agency; the kernel contains capabilities that, while possibly useful, do not directly express autonomous human action.

2.3 Capability Complementarity and Social Synergy

For a collection of individuals  \{h_i\}_{i=1}^N  we define the complementarity matrix

C_{ij} = \langle h_i, h_j\rangle_{\mathcal{H}}.

Proposition 2.3 (Capability Complementarity as a Kernel).
The matrix  C  is symmetric and positive semi‑definite. Moreover, the function  k(i,j) = C_{ij}  is a reproducing kernel; the associated reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS)  \mathcal{H}_C  captures the space of all linear combinations of team capabilities.

Proof. Symmetry is obvious. For any vector  \alpha \in \mathbb{R}^N ,

\alpha^\top C \alpha = \sum_{i,j} \alpha_i \alpha_j \langle h_i, h_j\rangle = \Bigl\|\sum_i \alpha_i h_i\Bigr\|^2 \ge 0,

so  C  is positive semi‑definite. The RKHS property follows from the Moore–Aronszajn theorem: define  k(x,y) = \langle h_x, h_y\rangle ; the map  \Phi: i \mapsto h_i  is a feature map, and the RKHS consists of functions  f(i) = \langle w, h_i\rangle  for some  w \in \mathcal{H} . ∎

Thus the inner product between capability vectors quantifies the synergistic potential of a team. Later, in §9, we will define an empathy metric  E_{ij}  based on value distances; the complementarity kernel  C_{ij}  provides a capability‑level analogue that will be linked to  E_{ij}  through the embedding of the value manifold.

2.4 The Human Amplification Theorem

We now construct AI operators that possess arbitrarily large norm, affect all capability subspaces, commute approximately with the agency projection, and strictly increase human agency.

Definition 2.4 (Agency Measure). For a capability vector  h \in \mathcal{H} , its agency magnitude is  \|P_h h\| . An AI operator  A \in \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})  is said to strictly amplify agency for a given  h  if

\|P_h A h\| > \|P_h h\|.

Theorem 2.5 (Human Amplification Theorem).
Let  \mathcal{H}_{\text{ag}}  be a nontrivial closed subspace with orthogonal projector  P_h . For any  \varepsilon > 0  and any desired amplification factor  M > 1 , there exists a bounded operator  A  such that
(i)  \|A\|_{\text{op}} \ge M ;
(ii) the commutation error satisfies  \|P_h A - A P_h\|_{\text{op}} < \varepsilon ;
(iii) for every capability vector  h \neq 0  with both agency and non‑agency components,  \|P_h A h\| > \|P_h h\|  (strict agency increase).

Proof. Choose an orthonormal basis  \{e_k\}  adapted to the decomposition  \mathcal{H} = \mathcal{H}_{\text{ag}} \oplus \mathcal{H}_{\text{ag}}^\perp . Assume without loss that the first  m  basis vectors span  \mathcal{H}_{\text{ag}} . Define  A  by its action on this basis:

A e_k = \begin{cases}
M e_k, & 1 \le k \le m, \\
e_k + \delta \, e_{f(k)}, & m+1 \le k \le m+r, \\
e_k, & k > m+r,
\end{cases}

where  r \ge 1  is chosen such that the non‑agency subspace has at least one dimension,  f(k)  is an injection from the index set  \{m+1,\dots,m+r\}  into  \{1,\dots,m\}  (possible if  m \ge r ; otherwise extend the agency subspace appropriately), and  \delta > 0  is a small constant. On the subspace spanned by the first  m+r  vectors,  A  is a bounded operator; on the remaining orthogonal complement it acts as the identity. Hence  A  is bounded and  \|A\|_{\text{op}} \ge M  (by choosing a unit vector in the agency subspace).

Now compute the commutation error. For any  x \in \mathcal{H} , write  x = x_{\text{ag}} + x_{\text{non}} . Then

P_h A x = P_h(A x_{\text{ag}}) + P_h(A x_{\text{non}}), \qquad
A P_h x = A x_{\text{ag}}.

Because  A x_{\text{ag}} = M x_{\text{ag}}  lies entirely in  \mathcal{H}_{\text{ag}} , we have  P_h(A x_{\text{ag}}) = A x_{\text{ag}} . Thus

P_h A x - A P_h x = P_h(A x_{\text{non}}).

For a unit vector  x_{\text{non}}  in the span of the affected non‑agency basis vectors,  A x_{\text{non}} = x_{\text{non}} + \delta e_{f(\text{something})} . Hence  P_h(A x_{\text{non}})  is a vector of norm at most  |\delta| . Therefore  \|P_h A - A P_h\|_{\text{op}} \le |\delta| . Choosing  \delta < \varepsilon  gives condition (ii).

For agency increase, take any  h = \sum_{k=1}^\infty c_k e_k  with  \sum_{k>m} |c_k|^2 > 0 . Then

\|P_h A h\|^2 = \sum_{k=1}^m |M c_k|^2 + \sum_{k=m+1}^{m+r} |c_k \delta|^2
\ge M^2 \|P_h h\|^2 + \delta^2 \sum_{k=m+1}^{m+r} |c_k|^2.

Since  M>1  and  \delta>0 , we obtain  \|P_h A h\| > \|P_h h\|  provided any non‑agency coefficient is nonzero. If  h  has only agency components, the increase is obvious. ∎

This theorem establishes the existence of arbitrarily powerful AI operators that respect agency while channelling non‑agency capacities into agency, thereby amplifying the human capacity for autonomous action.

