3. Manifold Representation of Human Value

3.1 The Value Manifold and Its Geometry

Human values are not linearly ordered; they exhibit curvature that captures trade‑offs and synergies. We model the space of coherent value configurations as a compact, connected,  d -dimensional Riemannian manifold  (\mathcal{V}, g) . To endow it with a purpose‑generating symplectic structure, we additionally equip it with a compatible almost complex structure  J  such that

g(JX, JY) = g(X,Y),

and the associated fundamental  2 -form  \omega(X,Y) = g(JX, Y)  is closed ( d\omega = 0 ). Thus  (\mathcal{V}, g, J)  is a compact almost Kähler manifold. Compactness guarantees that geodesic balls have finite volume and all later analytic properties hold. We do not assume that  J  is integrable (vanishing Nijenhuis tensor); none of the results in §5, §11, or §12 require integrability.

A point  v \in \mathcal{V}  represents an equivalence class of capability vectors that share the same value orientation. The metric tensor  g_{ab}  defines the length of a value change; geodesics are the paths of minimal value distortion. The Riemann curvature tensor  R^a_{\,bcd}  measures the failure of value directions to commute—a local measure of trade‑off.

Assumption 3.1 (Standing hypothesis).  (\mathcal{V}, g, J)  is a compact almost Kähler manifold.

Remark on existence. Not every manifold admits a compatible almost complex structure. A necessary condition is that  \mathcal{V}  be even‑dimensional; moreover, the tangent bundle must admit a complex vector bundle structure, which imposes topological restrictions (e.g., spheres  S^{2n}  admit an almost complex structure only for  n=1,3 ). This assumption therefore limits the class of admissible value manifolds. A full classification of such manifolds is left as Open Problem 6.

There exists an isometric embedding  \iota: \mathcal{V} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{H}  (Nash embedding theorem, adapted to the possibly infinite‑dimensional  \mathcal{H}  by using a sufficiently large Euclidean space and then mapping into  \mathcal{H}  via the basis), such that  g = \iota^* \langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_{\mathcal{H}} . The ambient Hilbert space thus contains the manifold as a submanifold, but the intrinsic geometry of values is non‑Euclidean.

3.2 Curvature Encodes Trade‑offs

For any pair of unit tangent vectors  u, v  at a point, the sectional curvature  \sec(u,v)  is given by  g(R(u,v)v,u) . We define the trade‑off coefficient

T_{ab} = -R_{acbd} u^c u^d,

where  u  is a direction of attempted simultaneous improvement of two values  a  and  b .

Lemma 3.2 (Curvature–Trade‑off Correspondence).
If  T_{ab} > 0  at a point, then any geodesic that attempts to increase both values  a  and  b  will necessarily decrease at least one of them; this is a tragic trade‑off. If  T_{ab} \le 0 , simultaneous improvement is geometrically possible (win–win).

Proof. The Jacobi equation for geodesic deviation shows that negative sectional curvature (positive  T_{ab} ) causes initially parallel value‑improvement directions to diverge, forcing one component to eventually decrease. Non‑positive curvature allows convergence. A full proof follows from the second variation formula of energy; see e.g. do Carmo (1992). ∎

Thus the sign of the curvature tensor components determines the local structure of value synergies.

3.3 The Flourishing Potential as a Morse Function

The flourishing potential  \Phi: \mathcal{V} \to \mathbb{R}  is a smooth function whose value at  v  measures the degree of human flourishing associated with that value configuration. We require  \Phi  to be a Morse function: all its critical points are non‑degenerate (the Hessian is non‑singular).

Proposition 3.3 (Flourishing Potential is Morse).
On the compact manifold  \mathcal{V} , a generic smooth function is Morse; that is, the set of critical points is finite and the Hessian at each critical point is non‑degenerate. We assume  \Phi  is such a Morse function. Its critical points are the flourishing attractors.

Proof. This is a standard consequence of the Morse lemma and the fact that Morse functions form an open dense subset of  C^\infty(\mathcal{V})  in the Whitney topology. ∎

3.4 Geodesic Value Learning

Value learning—the process by which an individual or society adjusts its values—can be modelled as a curve  v(t)  on  \mathcal{V}  that minimizes a cost functional trading off length and scalar curvature (value distortion):

\mathcal{E}[v] = \int_0^1 \bigl( \|\dot{v}(t)\|_g^2 + \lambda_1 \, R(v(t)) \bigr) \, dt,

where  R  is the scalar curvature and  \lambda_1  a parameter.

Theorem 3.4 (Geodesic Value Learning).
The minimizer of  \mathcal{E}[v]  subject to fixed endpoints converges, as  \lambda_1 \to 0 , to a geodesic of  (\mathcal{V}, g) . More precisely, if  \lambda_1  is small, the Euler–Lagrange equations approximate the geodesic equation; in the limit one recovers length‑minimizing geodesics.

Proof. The Euler–Lagrange equation for  \mathcal{E}  is

\nabla_{\dot{v}} \dot{v} = \lambda_1 \, \text{grad}\, R,

where  \nabla  is the Levi‑Civita connection. As  \lambda_1 \to 0 , the right‑hand side vanishes and we obtain the geodesic equation  \nabla_{\dot{v}}\dot{v} = 0 . Standard ODE perturbation theory guarantees that the solution depends continuously on  \lambda_1  and converges to a geodesic. ∎

Value learning in the absence of external forces thus follows the shortest path on the value manifold; curvature acts as a small bias.

