5. Dynamical Systems and Stability

5.1 Coupled Human–AI Dynamics

We model the joint evolution of human capabilities  h(t)  and AI capabilities  a(t)  by a controlled dynamical system on the product manifold  \mathcal{V} \times \mathcal{A} , or directly on  \mathcal{H} \times \mathcal{A} . Because the value manifold  \mathcal{V}  is compact, its tangent bundle is well‑behaved. The general form is

\dot{h} = f_h(h,a), \qquad \dot{a} = f_a(h,a) + u(t),

where  f_h, f_a  are smooth and  u(t)  is the alignment control. We assume the vector field  F(x) = (f_h, f_a)  is Lipschitz on every compact set.

Lemma 5.1 (Existence and Uniqueness of Solutions).
For any initial condition  (h(0), a(0))  and any locally Lipschitz control  u(t) , there exists a unique local solution to the coupled system. If the vector field is linearly bounded, the solution is global.

Proof. Standard Cauchy–Lipschitz theorem on Banach manifolds. ∎

5.2 Abundance Stability Theorem

In a post‑scarcity regime, material constraints vanish ( c_m \to 0 ). We study the autonomous dynamics on  \mathcal{V}  given by

\dot{v} = \nabla \Phi(v) + J \nabla \Phi(v),

where  J  is the almost complex structure of the almost Hermitian manifold. The term  \nabla \Phi  is the gradient with respect to  g , and  J\nabla\Phi  is an orthogonal, divergence‑free field that we call the purpose field.

Theorem 5.2 (Abundance Stability).
Consider the system

\dot{v} = \nabla \Phi(v) + J \nabla \Phi(v)

on the compact almost Kähler manifold  \mathcal{V} . Let  \Phi^* = \max_{v\in\mathcal{V}} \Phi(v) . Define the Lyapunov function  L(v) = \Phi^* - \Phi(v) . Then  \dot{L}(v) = -\|\nabla\Phi(v)\|^2 \le 0 . Consequently, every trajectory converges to the set of critical points of  \Phi . Moreover, any critical point at which the Hessian of  \Phi  is negative definite is a locally asymptotically stable equilibrium.

Proof. Using the chain rule and the metric compatibility of  J ,

\dot{L} = -\langle \nabla\Phi, \dot{v}\rangle = -\langle \nabla\Phi, \nabla\Phi + J\nabla\Phi\rangle = -\|\nabla\Phi\|^2 - \langle \nabla\Phi, J\nabla\Phi\rangle.

Since  J  is skew‑adjoint with respect to the Riemannian metric (i.e.,  g(JX,Y) = -g(X,JY) ), we have  g(\nabla\Phi, J\nabla\Phi) = 0 . Hence  \dot{L} = -\|\nabla\Phi\|^2 \le 0 . Because  L  is bounded below by  0  and  \mathcal{V}  is compact, LaSalle’s invariance principle implies that the  \omega -limit set of any trajectory is contained in the set where  \nabla\Phi = 0 . At a critical point where the Hessian  H(\Phi)  is negative definite, the linearization  \dot{\delta v} = H(\Phi) \delta v  has all eigenvalues with negative real part; thus the equilibrium is asymptotically stable. ∎

5.3 Bifurcation at Scarcity Zero

We now examine the effect of vanishing material constraints by adding a perturbation:

\dot{v} = \nabla\Phi(v) + c_m \Psi(v) + J\nabla\Phi(v),

where  \Psi  is a smooth vector field representing material incentives and  c_m \in \mathbb{R}  is a scalar control parameter.

Theorem 5.3 (Bifurcation at Scarcity Zero).
Assume that for  c_m > 0  the system has a family of equilibria  v^*(c_m)  that depends smoothly on  c_m , and that the linearization at these equilibria has a simple zero eigenvalue at  c_m = 0 . Under a generic transversality condition, the system undergoes a supercritical pitchfork bifurcation as  c_m \to 0 , exchanging stability from a scarcity‑driven attractor to the purpose‑driven attractor at  \nabla\Phi = 0 .

Proof. We apply the center manifold reduction. Let  x = v - v^*(0) . The dynamics can be written as

\dot{x} = L(c_m) x + N(x, c_m),

where  L(c_m)  is the linearization and  N  contains nonlinear terms. At  c_m = 0 , the linear operator  L(0)  has a simple zero eigenvalue (by assumption) with right eigenvector  \xi  and left eigenvector  \eta . All other eigenvalues lie in the open left half‑plane. By the center manifold theorem, there exists a one‑dimensional invariant manifold tangent to  \xi  on which the dynamics reduce to an ordinary differential equation for the amplitude  z \in \mathbb{R} :

\dot{z} = \alpha_1 c_m z + \alpha_2 z^3 + O(c_m^2 z, z^5),

where  \alpha_1 = \eta \cdot (\partial L/\partial c_m)(0) \xi  and  \alpha_2  is computed from the quadratic and cubic terms. The symmetry of the setup (no distinguished direction in the degenerate space) forces the absence of even‑order terms. For  \alpha_1 > 0  and  \alpha_2 < 0 , the trivial equilibrium  z=0  is stable for  c_m \le 0  and becomes unstable for  c_m > 0 , where two symmetric stable branches bifurcate. This is the supercritical pitchfork. The stable branch for  c_m = 0  is precisely the purpose‑driven attractor  \nabla\Phi = 0 . ∎

