6. Control Theory and Safe Takeoff

6.1 Controllability of the Alignment Linearization

Linearize the coupled dynamics around a desired equilibrium  (h^*, a^*) :

\dot{\delta x} = J \delta x + B u,

where  \delta x = (h - h^*, a - a^*) ,  J = \begin{pmatrix} \partial_h f_h & \partial_a f_h \\ \partial_h f_a & \partial_a f_a \end{pmatrix} , and  B  is the control input matrix.

Lemma 6.1 (Controllability of the Linearized System).
The pair  (J,B)  is controllable if and only if

\operatorname{rank} \bigl[ B, JB, J^2B, \dots, J^{2d-1}B \bigr] = 2d,

where  d  is the dimension of the state (finite‑dimensional approximation). For our choice of control input acting on the  a -dynamics, controllability holds generically provided the human and AI dynamics are sufficiently coupled.

Proof. Standard Kalman rank condition for linear time‑invariant systems. ∎

6.2 Lyapunov Alignment Controller

We propose the alignment control law

u(t) = -K_p \, \nabla_a S_{KL}(\rho \| \rho_0),

where  \rho  is the current value‑distribution induced by the AI and  \rho_0  is the human‑rooted prior, and  S_{KL}  is the Kullback–Leibler divergence. The prior  \rho_0  is assumed to be a fixed probability measure on  \mathcal{V}  that encodes baseline human values; its specification is a separate empirical problem.

Theorem 6.2 (Alignment Controller Stability).
With the above control law, the Lyapunov function

V = \frac{1}{2}\|h - h^*\|^2 + \frac{1}{2}\|a - a^*\|^2 + (\Phi^* - \Phi(h))

satisfies  \dot{V} \le 0 . If the system is zero‑state detectable, the equilibrium is asymptotically stable.

Proof. Compute the derivative:

\dot{V} = \langle h - h^*, \dot{h}\rangle + \langle a - a^*, \dot{a}\rangle - \langle \nabla\Phi, \dot{h}\rangle.

Using the dynamics  \dot{h} = f_h(h,a)  and  \dot{a} = f_a(h,a) - K_p \nabla_a S_{KL} , and the fact that near equilibrium  f_h(h,a) \approx -\gamma_h (h-h^*)  and  f_a(h,a) \approx -\gamma_a (a-a^*) , we obtain

\dot{V} \approx -\gamma_h \|h - h^*\|^2 - \gamma_a \|a - a^*\|^2 - K_p \langle a - a^*, \nabla_a S_{KL}\rangle - \|\nabla\Phi\|^2.

By design,  \nabla_a S_{KL}  points towards the prior, making  \langle a - a^*, \nabla_a S_{KL}\rangle \ge 0 . Hence the cross term is negative, yielding  \dot{V} \le 0 . Asymptotic stability follows from LaSalle’s principle if the only invariant set in the kernel of  \dot{V}  is the equilibrium. ∎

6.3 Spectral Safe‑Takeoff Condition

Theorem 6.3 (Safe Takeoff Spectral Condition).
Let  J \in \mathbb{R}^{2d \times 2d}  be the Jacobian of the coupled human–AI dynamics at the equilibrium, partitioned as

J = \begin{pmatrix} J_{hh} & J_{ha} \\ J_{ah} & J_{aa} \end{pmatrix}.

Assume  J_{hh}  and  J_{aa}  are diagonalizable and that the interactions  J_{ha}, J_{ah}  are bounded in spectral norm. A sufficient condition for the continuous‑time linearized system to be Hurwitz (all eigenvalues in the open left half‑plane) is

\|J_{ha}\|_2 \, \|J_{ah}\|_2 < \lambda_{\min}(-J_{hh}) \cdot \lambda_{\min}(-J_{aa}),

where  \lambda_{\min}(\cdot)  denotes the minimum eigenvalue of the symmetric part of the matrix (or the abscissa if not symmetric). In particular, if the product of the cross‑coupling strengths is smaller than the product of the self‑stabilization rates, safe takeoff is guaranteed.

Proof. Consider the block Schur complement of  J - sI . The characteristic polynomial is

\det(J_{hh} - sI) \det\bigl( (J_{aa} - sI) - J_{ah}(J_{hh} - sI)^{-1} J_{ha} \bigr).

For stability, we need all eigenvalues to have negative real parts. To bound the resolvent, assume without loss of generality that  J_{hh}  is normal (a typical property of gradient‑like systems near an equilibrium). Then for any  s  not in the spectrum,

\|(J_{hh} - sI)^{-1}\|_2 = \frac{1}{\operatorname{dist}(s, \sigma(J_{hh}))}.

If all eigenvalues of  J_{hh}  have real part  \le -\alpha_h  with  \alpha_h = \lambda_{\min}(-J_{hh}) > 0 , then for any  s  with  \operatorname{Re}(s) \ge -\alpha_h + \epsilon  we have  \|(J_{hh} - sI)^{-1}\|_2 \le 1/\epsilon . A similar bound holds for  J_{aa} .

Now consider the determinant of the Schur complement. By the block Gershgorin theorem applied to the spectral norm, any eigenvalue  s  of  J  must satisfy either

\| (J_{hh} - sI)^{-1} \|_2^{-1} \le \|J_{ha}\|_2 \quad \text{or} \quad \| (J_{aa} - sI)^{-1} \|_2^{-1} \le \|J_{ah}\|_2.

Suppose there is an eigenvalue with  \operatorname{Re}(s) \ge 0 . Then, because  J_{hh}  is stable, the first condition implies  \operatorname{dist}(s, \sigma(J_{hh})) \le \|J_{ha}\|_2 . The distance from  s  to the spectrum of  J_{hh}  is at least  \operatorname{Re}(s) + \alpha_h  (since the closest point lies on the line  \operatorname{Re} = -\alpha_h ), giving  \operatorname{Re}(s) + \alpha_h \le \|J_{ha}\|_2 . Similarly, the second condition yields  \operatorname{Re}(s) + \alpha_a \le \|J_{ah}\|_2  with  \alpha_a = \lambda_{\min}(-J_{aa}) .

If  \|J_{ha}\|_2 \|J_{ah}\|_2 < \alpha_h \alpha_a , then these inequalities cannot hold simultaneously for any  \operatorname{Re}(s) \ge 0  (by multiplying the two inequalities,  (\operatorname{Re}(s)+\alpha_h)(\operatorname{Re}(s)+\alpha_a) \le \|J_{ha}\|_2 \|J_{ah}\|_2 < \alpha_h\alpha_a , impossible when  \operatorname{Re}(s)\ge 0 ). Hence all eigenvalues have negative real part. ∎

