7. Game Theory and Civilization Equilibrium

We model the interaction of a large population of humans and an AI as a generalized potential game. Each human  i  chooses a value configuration  v_i  in a compact convex subset  \mathcal{V}_i \subset \mathcal{V}  (we can take  \mathcal{V}_i = \mathcal{V}  for simplicity). The AI chooses an amplification operator  A  from a compact set of admissible operators  \mathcal{A}_{\text{amp}}  (the class of operators satisfying Theorem 2.5’s near‑commutation bound). The resulting distribution of values is the empirical measure  \rho = \frac{1}{N} \sum_i \delta_{v_i} .

To couple the AI’s amplification to human flourishing, we introduce the realized amplification ratio for each individual  i  with capability vector  h_i :

a_i(A) = \frac{\|P_h A h_i\|}{\|P_h h_i\|} - 1 \ge 0,

which is nonnegative on  \mathcal{A}_{\text{amp}}  by Theorem 2.5(iii). Let  \chi: \mathcal{V} \to [0,\infty)  be a smooth receptivity function that quantifies how much a chosen value orientation benefits from amplified agency (e.g.,  \chi(v) = \langle \operatorname{grad}\Phi(v), \xi\rangle_+  for a reference direction, or  \chi \equiv 1  for uniform receptivity).

The civilization objective function is

U_c(v_1,\dots,v_N, A) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N \Bigl(\Phi(v_i) + \beta\, a_i(A)\,\chi(v_i)\Bigr) - \lambda_2\, S_{KL}(\rho_A \| \rho_0),

where  \rho_A  is the value distribution induced by applying  A  (a smooth push‑forward of the empirical measure),  \beta > 0  weighs the amplification benefit, and  \lambda_2 > 0  penalizes divergence from the human‑rooted prior  \rho_0 .

Lemma 7.1′ (Potential Game Structure).
Define the potential function

\Psi'(v_1,\dots,v_N, A) = \sum_{i=1}^N \Bigl(\Phi(v_i) + \beta\, a_i(A)\,\chi(v_i)\Bigr) - N \lambda_2\, S_{KL}(\rho_A \| \rho_0).

Then the payoff of player  i  can be taken as

u_i(v_i, v_{-i}, A) = \Phi(v_i) + \beta\, a_i(A)\,\chi(v_i) - \lambda_2\, S_{KL}(\rho_A \| \rho_0).

Since  u_i = \Psi' - \sum_{j \neq i} \bigl[\Phi(v_j) + \beta a_j(A)\chi(v_j)\bigr]  and the subtracted term is independent of  v_i , the augmented game remains an exact potential game with potential  \Psi' .

Proof. Immediate from the definition. ∎

Theorem 7.2′ (Civilization Equilibrium, amplification‑coupled).
Assume  \mathcal{A}_{\text{amp}}  is compact in operator norm and  A \mapsto a_i(A)  is continuous for each  i  (true by construction). Then a pure Nash equilibrium  (v_1^*, \dots, v_N^*, A^*)  exists, obtained as a global maximizer of  \Psi'  over the compact set  \mathcal{V}^N \times \mathcal{A}_{\text{amp}} . Moreover, this equilibrium is non‑trivial ( A^* \neq I ) whenever the amplification benefit outweighs the alignment cost:

\beta \sup_A \sum_i a_i(A)\chi(v_i^*) > \inf_A N \lambda_2 \bigl[ S_{KL}(\rho_A \| \rho_0) - S_{KL}(\rho_I \| \rho_0) \bigr].

Proof.  \Psi'  is continuous on a compact strategy space ( \Phi, a_i, \chi  are smooth/continuous; the KL divergence is lower semicontinuous and bounded). Hence it attains a global maximum at some  (v^*, A^*) , which is a Nash equilibrium of the potential game by Lemma 7.1′. For the non‑triviality claim, compare  \Psi'(v^*, I)  against  \Psi'(v^*, A)  for the maximizing  A ; the inequality ensures  \Psi'(v^*, A) > \Psi'(v^*, I) , so no maximizer can be the identity. ∎

Proposition 7.4 (Price of Anarchy bound).
Assume that the KL divergence satisfies a quadratic growth condition with respect to the Wasserstein distance:  S_{KL}(\rho_A \| \rho_0) \ge \frac{\kappa}{2} W_2^2(\rho_A, \rho_0)  for some  \kappa > 0  (e.g., when  \rho_0  is strongly log‑concave). Let  L_\Phi  be the Lipschitz constant of  \Phi  on  \mathcal{V} . Then for any Nash equilibrium  (v^N, A^N) ,

U_c(v^N, A^N) \ge \max_{v,A} U_c(v,A) - L_\Phi \sqrt{\frac{2}{\kappa \lambda_2}} - \frac{C}{\sqrt{N}},

where  C  depends on the diameter of  \mathcal{V}  and the amplification bounds. In particular, as the alignment penalty weight  \lambda_2  grows, the inefficiency (gap to global optimum) decays as  1/\sqrt{\lambda_2} .

Proof sketch. The global optimum is approximated by a configuration where all  v_i  are at a global maximizer of  \Phi  and  \rho_A  is close to  \rho_0  (hence  W_2 \approx 0 ). For the Nash equilibrium, the first‑order conditions and the KL growth bound force  W_2(\rho_{A^N}, \rho_0) \le \frac{2L_\Phi}{\kappa \lambda_2} + o(1) . Using the Lipschitz property of  \Phi , the difference in the sum of  \Phi  values is at most  L_\Phi W_2(\rho_{A^N}, \rho_0) , yielding the stated bound. The  1/\sqrt{N}  term accounts for finite‑sample fluctuations of the empirical measure. A full variational proof appears in Appendix B. ∎

