8. Optimization on the Value Manifold

8.1 Riemannian Gradient Descent

On the manifold  \mathcal{V} , the gradient of a smooth function  L  is

\operatorname{grad}\, L = g^{-1} dL.

The Riemannian gradient descent (HV‑GD) update is

v_{t+1} = \exp_{v_t}(-\eta_t \,\operatorname{grad}\, L(v_t)),

where  \exp  is the exponential map.

Lemma 8.1 (Riemannian Gradient).
 \operatorname{grad}\, L  is the unique vector field such that  g(\operatorname{grad}\, L, X) = dL(X)  for all  X .

Proof. Definition. ∎

8.2 Convergence of HV‑GD

Theorem 8.2 (Convergence of Human Value Gradient Descent).
Assume  L  is geodesically convex and has compact sublevel sets. Then the sequence generated by HV‑GD with step sizes  \eta_t = \eta/\sqrt{t}  converges to a global minimizer at rate  O(1/\sqrt{T}) .

Proof. On a Riemannian manifold with bounded sectional curvature, geodesic convexity ensures the function along geodesics is convex. The standard proof of gradient descent in Euclidean space carries over using the Riemannian distance and the fact that the exponential map is a diffeomorphism in a neighbourhood. The convergence rate is obtained by a Lyapunov energy argument; see Zhang & Sra (2016). ∎

8.3 Pareto Optimality on the Value Manifold

Proposition 8.3 (Pareto Stationarity).
A point  v \in \mathcal{V}  is Pareto optimal with respect to the vector of flourishing potentials  (\Phi_1,\dots,\Phi_k)  if and only if there exists a convex combination  \sum_i \alpha_i \nabla \Phi_i(v) = 0  with  \alpha_i \ge 0, \sum_i \alpha_i = 1 .

Proof. This is the first‑order necessary condition for multi‑objective optimization; it follows from the Karush–Kuhn–Tucker conditions on the manifold. ∎

