9. Network Theory and Social Value Dynamics

9.1 The Empathy Graph

The human population forms a weighted graph  G=(V,E,w)  where vertices are individuals and edge weights are the empathy metric

E_{ij} = \exp\!\Bigl(-\frac{d_\mathcal{V}(v_i,v_j)^2}{2\sigma^2}\Bigr).

The weighted graph Laplacian is

L_{ij} = \delta_{ij} \sum_{k} E_{ik} - E_{ij}.

Lemma 9.1 (Empathy Laplacian Properties).
 L  is symmetric and positive semi‑definite. Its smallest eigenvalue is  0  with eigenvector  \mathbf{1} ; the second eigenvalue  \lambda_2(L)  (algebraic connectivity) measures the speed of value diffusion across the network.

Proof. Standard spectral graph theory. ∎

The empathy weights  E_{ij}  are related to the capability complementarity  C_{ij}  of §2.3: if the embedding  \iota: \mathcal{V} \hookrightarrow \mathcal{H}  is approximately isometric, then  d_\mathcal{V}(v_i,v_j)^2 \approx \|h_i - h_j\|^2 . Hence  E_{ij} \approx \exp(-\|h_i-h_j\|^2/2\sigma^2) , which decays with decreasing complementarity. The two synergy notions are therefore compatible.

9.2 Value Consensus with Riemannian Gradient Flow

We model consensus on the value manifold by the gradient flow of the potential

\Psi(v_1,\dots,v_N) = \frac{1}{2} \sum_{i,j} E_{ij} \, d_\mathcal{V}^2(v_i, v_j) + \sum_i \Phi(v_i).

The gradient with respect to  v_i  is  \nabla_{v_i} \Psi = \sum_j E_{ij} \,\text{grad}_{v_i} \bigl(\frac{1}{2} d_\mathcal{V}^2(v_i, v_j)\bigr) + \operatorname{grad}\,\Phi(v_i) . Since  \operatorname{grad}_{v_i} \frac{1}{2} d_\mathcal{V}^2(v_i, v_j) = -\log_{v_i}(v_j)  (the negative of the Riemannian logarithm map), the consensus dynamics become

\dot{v}_i = -\sum_j E_{ij} \log_{v_i}(v_j) + \operatorname{grad}\,\Phi(v_i).

Theorem 9.2 (Riemannian Value Consensus).
Assume the graph is connected and that  \Phi  has a local maximum  v^*  with basin of attraction  B . If the initial values  v_i(0)  all lie in  B  and the largest eigenvalue of the Hessian of  \Phi  at  v^*  (in absolute value) is smaller than  \lambda_2(L) , then the consensus dynamics converge exponentially to the synchronized configuration where all  v_i = v^* .

Proof. The function  \Psi  is a Lyapunov function; its Hessian at the synchronized state  (v^*, \dots, v^*)  is  L \otimes I_d + \operatorname{diag}(\nabla^2\Phi(v^*)) . The first term has smallest positive eigenvalue  \lambda_2(L)  on the subspace orthogonal to consensus. Adding the Hessian of  \Phi  preserves positive definiteness if  \|\nabla^2\Phi(v^*)\| < \lambda_2(L) . Exponential stability follows. ∎

Remark 9.3 (Multiple attractors). Since  \Phi  is Morse with potentially several local maxima, the value manifold splits into basins of attraction. Each basin corresponds to a distinct consensus cluster; a civilization may therefore exhibit multiple stable value equilibria. The pitchfork bifurcation of §5.3 creates two symmetric local maxima, consistent with this picture.

9.3 Resilience to Node Removal

Theorem 9.4 (Social Resilience).
If the algebraic connectivity  \lambda_2 > \delta , the network remains connected after removal of up to  k < \lambda_2 / d_{\max}  nodes, where  d_{\max}  is maximum degree.

Proof. By the eigenvalue interlacing theorem, removing a node reduces  \lambda_2  by at most the degree of the removed node. The bound follows. ∎

