10. Category Theory and Alignment Architecture

10.1 Categories of Humans and AI

Define Hum as the category whose objects are human capability vectors  h \in \mathcal{H} , and morphisms  h_1 \to h_2  are empathy‑weighted influence maps. AI is the category whose objects are AI capability operators  A \in \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{H})  and morphisms are operator compositions.

Definition 10.1. A functor  F: \text{Hum} \to \text{AI}  is called an amplification functor if it maps each human to an AI operator that amplifies agency (as in Theorem 2.5).

Proposition 10.2 (Amplification Functor).
The functor defined by  h \mapsto A_h , where  A_h = I + \alpha (h \otimes h) P_h , is faithful but not full. It preserves distinct human identities but creates new AI‑to‑AI morphisms that have no human counterpart.

Proof. Faithfulness means it is injective on hom‑sets; because different humans map to different operators, it holds. Fullness would require that every AI morphism arises from a human morphism, which is false—AI can compose operators in ways that do not correspond to human empathy links. ∎

10.3 Alignment as a Natural Transformation

Alignment condition  [A, P_h] = 0  can be expressed categorically.

Theorem 10.3 (Alignment as Natural Transformation).
There exists a natural transformation  \eta: \text{Id}_{\text{Hum}} \Rightarrow P_h \circ F  if and only if the amplification functor commutes with the agency projection, i.e.,  P_h \circ F = F \circ P_h .

Proof. The naturality square for any morphism  m: h_1 \to h_2  requires  P_h(F(m)) \circ \eta_{h_1} = \eta_{h_2} \circ m . This is precisely the condition that  F  intertwines with  P_h . ∎

Thus alignment is a structural compatibility between the human and AI categories.

