11. Probabilistic Analysis and Concentration of Flourishing

11.1 Fokker–Planck Equation for Value Distribution

When stochastic noise is present, the density  \rho(v,t)  of values evolves according to

\partial_t \rho = -\nabla \cdot (\rho P) + D \Delta \rho,

where  P = \nabla\Phi + J\nabla\Phi  is the purpose field and  D  a diffusion constant.

Lemma 11.1 (Fokker–Planck Evolution).
The stationary distribution is  \rho_\infty \propto \exp(\Phi/D)  (the Gibbs measure) if  J\nabla\Phi = 0 ; with the full purpose field it is a steady state of the advection–diffusion equation.

Proof. Standard derivation from Itô diffusion. ∎

11.2 Concentration of Flourishing

Theorem 11.2 (Concentration of Flourishing).
If  \Phi  is  L -Lipschitz on the compact manifold  \mathcal{V}  (with respect to the geodesic distance), then for any probability measure  \mu  on  \mathcal{V} ,

\mathbb{P}[|\Phi(v) - \mathbb{E}_\mu\Phi| > t] \le 2 \exp\!\Bigl(-\frac{t^2}{2 L^2}\Bigr).

Proof. This follows from the Gaussian concentration inequality for Lipschitz functions on compact Riemannian manifolds, which holds because such manifolds satisfy the log‑Sobolev inequality with a constant depending on the Ricci curvature. ∎

Thus in a well‑aligned civilization, extreme deviations from the flourishing attractor are exponentially rare.

