12. Symplectic Geometry and Conservation Laws

12.1 Purpose Field as a Hamiltonian Perturbation

The compact almost Kähler manifold  \mathcal{V}  carries the symplectic form  \omega = g(J\cdot, \cdot) . The vector field  J\nabla\Phi  is the Hamiltonian vector field of  \Phi  with respect to  \omega , i.e.,  \omega(J\nabla\Phi, \cdot) = d\Phi  (up to a sign convention). We define the purpose field as  X_\Phi = J\nabla\Phi .

Lemma 12.1 (Liouville’s Theorem).
The flow of  X_\Phi  preserves the Liouville measure  \omega^d  (the symplectic volume form). Hence  \nabla \cdot X_\Phi = 0 .

Proof.  \mathcal{L}_{X_\Phi} \omega = d(i_{X_\Phi}\omega) + i_{X_\Phi}d\omega = d(d\Phi) = 0 , since  d\omega = 0 . ∎

12.2 Purpose Conservation Principle

Theorem 12.2 (Purpose Conservation Principle).
Let  \dim \mathcal{V} = 2n ,  \nu = \omega^n  the Liouville volume form, and  \Sigma \subset \mathcal{V}  a closed  (2n-1) -dimensional submanifold without boundary. Let  \phi_t  be the flow of the total field  P = \nabla\Phi + X_\Phi ,  X_\Phi = J\nabla\Phi . Define the flux

\mathcal{F}(t) = \oint_{\phi_t(\Sigma)} i_{X_\Phi} \nu.

Then  \mathcal{F}(t)  is constant in  t : purpose flux is conserved under the full coupled dynamics, not just the solenoidal component.

Proof. By the transport formula,

\frac{d}{dt}\Big|_{t=0} \mathcal{F}(t) = \oint_\Sigma \mathcal{L}_P (i_{X_\Phi} \nu).

Split  \mathcal{L}_P = \mathcal{L}_{X_\Phi} + \mathcal{L}_{\nabla\Phi} .

· Step 1 ( X_\Phi  part). Since  \mathcal{L}_{X_\Phi} \omega = 0 , we have  \mathcal{L}_{X_\Phi} \nu = 0 . Using the identity  \mathcal{L}_{X_\Phi}(i_{X_\Phi} \nu) = i_{X_\Phi}(\mathcal{L}_{X_\Phi} \nu) + i_{[X_\Phi, X_\Phi]} \nu = 0 , the term vanishes.
· Step 2 ( \nabla\Phi  part). Because  \nu  is a top‑degree form,  d\nu = 0 . Also  d(i_{X_\Phi} \nu) = \mathcal{L}_{X_\Phi} \nu - i_{X_\Phi}(d\nu) = 0 , so  i_{X_\Phi} \nu  is closed. Apply Cartan’s formula to  \nabla\Phi :

\mathcal{L}_{\nabla\Phi} (i_{X_\Phi} \nu) = d\bigl( i_{\nabla\Phi} i_{X_\Phi} \nu \bigr) + i_{\nabla\Phi}\bigl( d(i_{X_\Phi} \nu) \bigr) = d\beta,

where  \beta = i_{\nabla\Phi} i_{X_\Phi} \nu  is a  (2n-2) -form. Hence  \mathcal{L}_{\nabla\Phi}(i_{X_\Phi} \nu)  is exact.

· Step 3 (Stokes). Combining,  \mathcal{L}_P(i_{X_\Phi} \nu) = d\beta  is exact. Pulling back by  \phi_t  preserves exactness, and since  \Sigma  has no boundary,  \oint_\Sigma d(\phi_t^* \beta) = 0 . Therefore  \frac{d}{dt}\mathcal{F}(t) = 0  for all  t . ∎

This formalizes the intuition that meaning, encoded in the purpose flux, cannot be destroyed—only redistributed.

12.3 Human Identity Invariance

Theorem 12.3 (Identity Invariance under Amplification).
Let the identity subspace  \mathcal{H}_{\text{id}}  be the subset of capabilities whose norm remains bounded under amplification, and suppose the value configuration  v(t)  evolves under the coupled dynamics of §5.2, which includes the restoring gradient  \nabla\Phi . If the initial value point is within the basin of attraction of a local maximum  v^*  of  \Phi  that corresponds to the human’s core identity, then  v(t)  remains within a bounded geodesic distance from  v^*  for all time. In particular, identity does not diverge.

Proof. The Lyapunov function  L(v) = \Phi^* - \Phi(v)  satisfies  \dot{L} \le 0 . Hence  \Phi(v(t))  is non‑decreasing and bounded above by  \Phi^* . Since  \mathcal{V}  is compact and  \Phi  is smooth, the set  \{v: \Phi(v) \ge \Phi(v(0))\}  is compact; the trajectory stays in this set. The distance from  v^*  is bounded by the diameter of the basin. ∎

