13. Thermodynamic Constraints on Civilization

13.1 Entropy Budget of the Human–AI Ecosystem

The total entropy of the civilization,  S_{\text{civ}} , satisfies

\dot{S}_{\text{civ}} = \dot{S}_{\text{int}} - \Psi_{\text{export}},

where  \Psi_{\text{export}} = \langle a, \nabla S \rangle  is the entropy export rate through AI‑optimized resource flows.

Proposition 13.1 (Entropy Budget).
The civilization must export at least as much entropy as it produces internally to avoid collapse:  \Psi_{\text{export}} \ge \dot{S}_{\text{int}} .

Proof. For the system to maintain a steady state far from equilibrium, the entropy production inside must be balanced by export. ∎

13.2 Sustainability Condition

Theorem 13.2 (Sustainability Condition).
A civilization is sustainable if and only if

\lim_{t\to\infty} \dot{S}_{\text{int}} = 0

and there exists a stationary AI policy  a^*  such that  \Psi_{\text{export}}(a^*) \ge \dot{S}_{\text{int}}  holds at all times.

Proof. If internal entropy production does not asymptotically vanish, the system will eventually exceed any finite export capacity. The condition is necessary and sufficient for the existence of a non‑degenerate steady state. ∎

