14.1 von Neumann–Morgenstern Axioms

Lemma 14.1 (VNM Representation).
The civilization utility  U_c  satisfies the von Neumann–Morgenstern axioms if  \Phi  is continuous and  \rho  is countably additive. Then  U_c  is an expected utility function.

Proof. The KL divergence term is a penalty added to the linear expected utility; the combined functional remains affine in the probabilities, hence it inherits the VNM properties. ∎

14.2 Exponential Suppression of Value Drift

Theorem 14.2 (Exponential Convergence to Human‑Rooted Prior).
Under the alignment control law  u(t) = -K_p \nabla_a S_{KL}(\rho \| \rho_0) , the KL divergence decays exponentially:

S_{KL}(\rho(t) \| \rho_0) \le S_{KL}(\rho(0) \| \rho_0) \, e^{-\lambda_3 t},

for some  \lambda_3 > 0 . Consequently, the value distribution never drifts far from the human‑rooted baseline.

Proof. The time derivative of the KL divergence along the flow is

\frac{d}{dt} S_{KL}(\rho \| \rho_0) = -\langle \nabla_a S_{KL}, \dot{a} \rangle = -K_p \|\nabla_a S_{KL}\|^2 \le 0.

With additional convexity and a Polyak–Łojasiewicz condition, one obtains a linear convergence rate. ∎

