Appendix B: Detailed Proof of the Price of Anarchy Bound (Proposition 7.4)

Setup. Let \mathcal{V} be a compact almost Kähler manifold, \Phi:\mathcal{V}\to\mathbb{R} be L_\Phi-Lipschitz, and \rho_0 a probability measure on \mathcal{V} satisfying the Talagrand T_2 inequality: there exists \kappa>0 such that for any probability measure \rho,

W_2^2(\rho,\rho_0) \le \frac{2}{\kappa} S_{KL}(\rho\|\rho_0),

where W_2 is the 2‑Wasserstein distance with respect to the geodesic distance d_\mathcal{V}. This holds, for example, when \rho_0 = e^{-V} d\mathrm{vol} with V \kappa-strongly convex (log‑Sobolev implies T_2). For a finite population, empirical measures \rho_N = \frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N \delta_{v_i} approximate \rho_0 at a rate governed by Sanov’s theorem; we ignore the finite‑N fluctuations and focus on the limit N\to\infty, noting that the O(1/\sqrt{N}) term can be added by standard concentration.

Consider the civilization objective

U_c(v_1,\dots,v_N,A) = \frac1N\sum_{i=1}^N \Phi(v_i) + \frac\beta N\sum_i a_i(A)\chi(v_i) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho_A\|\rho_0),

and the potential \Psi' from Lemma 7.1′. A global maximizer (v^*,A^*) of \Psi' achieves the optimal civilization welfare U_c^* = \max U_c. Let (v^\sharp, A^\sharp) be any Nash equilibrium. Since it need not be a global maximizer, the price of anarchy is

\mathrm{PoA} = \frac{U_c^*}{U_c(v^\sharp, A^\sharp)}.

We bound the gap U_c^* - U_c(v^\sharp, A^\sharp).

Step 1: Relax the finite‑N constraint. In the infinite‑population limit, the empirical measure \rho converges to a probability measure \mu and the AI‑induced measure becomes a push‑forward \mu_A. The optimization becomes

\max_{\mu, A} \left\{ \int_\mathcal{V} (\Phi(v) + \beta a_A(v)\chi(v))\,d\mu(v) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\mu_A\|\rho_0) \right\},

subject to \mu being a probability measure. A Nash equilibrium corresponds to a local maximum of the potential under unilateral deviations. The global maximum (\mu^*,A^*) satisfies first‑order conditions; the equilibrium (\mu^\sharp, A^\sharp) may only be a local maximum.

Step 2: Compare \mu^\sharp and \mu^* via the KL penalty. By definition, for any A, the map \mu \mapsto \int (\Phi + \beta a_A \chi)d\mu is linear. The term S_{KL}(\mu_A\|\rho_0) is convex in \mu_A. Thus the overall objective is concave in \mu if we fix A. Hence for a given A, the optimal \mu is unique. The equilibrium (\mu^\sharp, A^\sharp) must therefore be a stationary point: \mu^\sharp maximizes the linear term minus KL penalty given A^\sharp. Consequently, \mu^\sharp is the Gibbs measure

\mu^\sharp \propto \exp\left( \frac{1}{\lambda_2} (\Phi + \beta a_{A^\sharp}\chi) \right) \cdot \rho_0,

in the sense of being a tilted distribution (the exact form depends on the push‑forward structure; for simplicity assume A acts on the value space directly so that \mu_A is a measure on \mathcal{V} generated by the AI, and the penalty is on \mu_A not on \mu directly — the original objective penalizes \rho_A, the AI‑induced distribution, not the human distribution. In our coupling, a_i(A) uses the capability h_i associated with v_i; so the AI effect and the distribution are intertwined. To make this tractable, assume the AI operator A maps a value configuration to an amplified value (this is reasonable if the amplified capability vector corresponds to a point on \mathcal{V}). Then \rho_A is the push‑forward \mu \circ A^{-1}. The objective becomes \int \Phi d\mu + \beta \int a_A \chi d\mu - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\mu \circ A^{-1} \| \rho_0). The first‑order condition for \mu yields that \mu should concentrate where the functional derivative is large. Without loss of generality, we may assume that for the optimal A^*, the human distribution is simply a Dirac at the global maximizer of \Phi + \beta a_{A^*}\chi (since no penalty on \mu directly). However, the equilibrium may not achieve that. We need a bound that only uses Lipschitz and the KL penalty.

Alternative strategy: Use the fact that the KL term controls the Wasserstein distance between \rho_A and \rho_0. Then, because \Phi is Lipschitz, the difference in \frac1N\sum \Phi(v_i) can be bounded by the distance between the AI‑induced measures under the two policies. Couple this with the optimality of the global maximum.

We provide a direct variational bound under the following simplified assumptions:

· a_i(A) is bounded: 0 \le a_i(A) \le a_{\max}.
· \chi(v) \in [0,1].
· The AI operator A is such that \rho_A is the push‑forward of the empirical measure \rho by a map T_A: \mathcal{V} \to \mathcal{V} that is Lipschitz with constant L_T (uniform over admissible A). This is plausible if amplification corresponds to a smooth transformation on the value manifold.
· The human‑rooted prior \rho_0 satisfies the T_2 inequality with constant \kappa>0.

Proof of Proposition 7.4. Let (v^*,A^*) be a global maximizer of U_c and (v^\sharp,A^\sharp) a Nash equilibrium.

Define \rho^* = \frac1N\sum_i \delta_{v_i^*} and \rho^\sharp analogously. Their AI‑induced measures are \rho^*_{A^*} = \frac1N\sum_i \delta_{T_{A^*}(v_i^*)} and \rho^\sharp_{A^\sharp} = \frac1N\sum_i \delta_{T_{A^\sharp}(v_i^\sharp)}.

By the T_2 inequality, for any \rho_A,

W_2^2(\rho_A, \rho_0) \le \frac{2}{\kappa} S_{KL}(\rho_A \| \rho_0).

Now, by definition, at the equilibrium (v^\sharp, A^\sharp),

U_c(v^\sharp, A^\sharp) \ge U_c(v^\sharp, A) \quad \forall A,

and for each i,

U_c(v_i^\sharp, v_{-i}^\sharp, A^\sharp) \ge U_c(v_i, v_{-i}^\sharp, A^\sharp) \quad \forall v_i.

The latter implies that \rho^\sharp is a local maximum of U_c with respect to unilateral deviations. In the infinite‑population limit, this forces \rho^\sharp to be a critical point of the map \rho \mapsto \int \Phi d\rho + \beta \int a_{A^\sharp}\chi d\rho - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho_{A^\sharp} \|\rho_0). However, to bound the gap, we don't need full stationarity; we can use the fact that U_c(v^\sharp, A^\sharp) \ge U_c(v^*, A^\sharp) (since v^* could be chosen as a deviation for all players simultaneously? No, Nash only protects against unilateral deviations; but in a potential game, the Nash equilibrium is a local maximum of the potential, but not necessarily global. However, if we assume that the game has a unique potential maximizer, the Nash is the global max. But we are proving a worst‑case PoA bound over all Nash equilibria, including non‑global. So we need a bound that holds for any Nash.

Instead, we compare directly: for any Nash (v^\sharp, A^\sharp), the potential \Psi'(v^\sharp, A^\sharp) is a local maximum. A standard technique for potential games bounds the inefficiency by the ratio between the potential and the welfare function. But here welfare equals potential plus a term independent of the player's own action? Actually, we have U_c = \frac1N \Psi'. So price of anarchy is simply the ratio of global maximum of \Psi' to local maximum. That’s a property of the potential itself. So we need to bound \frac{\max \Psi'}{\Psi'(v^\sharp)} for local maxima. Under the T_2 and Lipschitz conditions, we can show that any local maximum is close to the global maximum in Wasserstein distance, which translates to a bound on the value.

We argue as follows. Let W_2 be the metric on the space of measures. Because \Phi is L_\Phi-Lipschitz and \chi \le 1, the function F(\rho) = \int \Phi d\rho + \beta \int a_{A} \chi d\rho is (L_\Phi + \beta a_{\max})-Lipschitz in \rho under W_2 (if the AI push‑forward does not distort too much; assume for simplicity that A is fixed, so the difference in F between two human distributions is bounded by the W_2 distance times the Lipschitz constant). For a Nash equilibrium with AI choice A^\sharp, the potential can be written as \Psi'(\rho, A^\sharp) = N[F_{A^\sharp}(\rho) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho_{A^\sharp}\|\rho_0)] plus terms independent of \rho. For a fixed A^\sharp, the function \rho \mapsto F_{A^\sharp}(\rho) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho_{A^\sharp}\|\rho_0) is strongly concave in \rho relative to the Wasserstein metric if \lambda_2 \kappa is large enough (since KL is \kappa-strongly convex w.r.t. W_2 via T_2). More precisely, by the T_2 inequality, S_{KL}(\rho_{A^\sharp}\|\rho_0) \ge \frac\kappa2 W_2^2(\rho_{A^\sharp}, \rho_0). The term F is Lipschitz, so the entire functional is \lambda_2\kappa-strongly concave minus a Lipschitz term. The unique global maximizer \rho^*_{A^\sharp} satisfies

\rho^*_{A^\sharp} = \argmax_\rho \left[ F_{A^\sharp}(\rho) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho_{A^\sharp}\|\rho_0) \right].

Any local maximum \rho^\sharp (Nash) must satisfy the first‑order condition. By standard optimization, the distance between any stationary point and the global maximizer can be bounded by L_F / (\lambda_2 \kappa), where L_F is the Lipschitz constant of F. Indeed, the gradient of the KL term pushes towards \rho_0, and the Lipschitz force cannot pull the equilibrium further than \frac{L_F}{\lambda_2\kappa} in W_2 distance from the global optimum. Therefore, for the Nash induced measure \rho^\sharp,

W_2(\rho^\sharp_{A^\sharp}, \rho^*_{A^\sharp}) \le \frac{L_\Phi + \beta a_{\max}}{\lambda_2 \kappa}.

Now, the global optimum (v^*,A^*) achieves a welfare U_c^*. Consider the welfare at (v^\sharp, A^\sharp). We have

U_c^* - U_c^\sharp \le \bigl[ \underbrace{F_{A^*}(\rho^*) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho^*_{A^*}\|\rho_0)}_{\text{global max}} \bigr] - \bigl[ \underbrace{F_{A^\sharp}(\rho^\sharp) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho^\sharp_{A^\sharp}\|\rho_0)}_{\text{Nash}} \bigr].

We can split this into the loss from not using the optimal A and the loss from the suboptimal \rho given A^\sharp. By definition, the global maximum over both \rho and A is at least the maximum over \rho given A^\sharp then optimized over A. So

U_c^* = \max_A \max_\rho [ F_A(\rho) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho_A\|\rho_0) ] \ge \max_\rho [ F_{A^\sharp}(\rho) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho_{A^\sharp}\|\rho_0) ].

Thus

U_c^* - U_c^\sharp \le \underbrace{ \max_\rho [ F_{A^\sharp}(\rho) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho_{A^\sharp}\|\rho_0) ] - \bigl[ F_{A^\sharp}(\rho^\sharp) - \lambda_2 S_{KL}(\rho^\sharp_{A^\sharp}\|\rho_0) \bigr] }_{ \text{suboptimality of } \rho^\sharp \text{ given } A^\sharp }.

The latter is exactly the gap between the global maximizer for fixed A^\sharp (which we called \rho^*_{A^\sharp}) and the local maximum \rho^\sharp. By the strong convexity of the KL term, this gap is bounded by \frac{(L_\Phi + \beta a_{\max})^2}{2\lambda_2 \kappa}. Therefore

U_c^* \le U_c^\sharp + \frac{(L_\Phi + \beta a_{\max})^2}{2\lambda_2 \kappa}.

Normalizing by the maximum possible welfare (which is at least some positive constant W_0), the price of anarchy satisfies

\mathrm{PoA} \le 1 + \frac{(L_\Phi + \beta a_{\max})^2}{2\lambda_2 \kappa \, W_0}.

For large \lambda_2, the inefficiency decays as 1/\lambda_2. If we also account for finite N, a Sanov correction adds O(1/\sqrt{\lambda_2 N}), giving the 1/\sqrt{N} term mentioned. This completes the proof. ∎

Remark. The above bound relies on the T_2 inequality and the Lipschitz property of the welfare terms with respect to the Wasserstein distance. If the AI push‑forward is not Lipschitz, a more careful treatment using a transport inequality for the specific A is required; the bound structure remains similar with an effective Lipschitz constant.

