Appendix C: Compactness and Continuity for the Civilization Game

To ensure that Theorem 7.2′ yields a pure Nash equilibrium, we must verify that the strategy space \mathcal{V}^N \times \mathcal{A}_{\text{amp}} is compact and the potential \Psi' is continuous.

· \mathcal{V} is a compact manifold by Assumption 3.1, hence \mathcal{V}^N is compact in the product topology.
· The set of admissible AI operators \mathcal{A}_{\text{amp}} must be defined so that it is compact in the operator norm topology (or at least sequentially compact) and the maps A \mapsto a_i(A) and A \mapsto \rho_A are continuous. A convenient construction: fix a finite‑dimensional truncation of \mathcal{H} to dimension D (large), which is sufficient for all practical purposes; the space of operators constructed in Theorem 2.5 with parameters M \in [1, M_{\max}] and \delta \in [0, \varepsilon] (for a given \varepsilon) is a compact subset of \mathbb{R}^{D \times D} under the usual topology, because it is the image of a closed bounded set under a continuous map. Taking \mathcal{A}_{\text{amp}} to be this set (or its closure) ensures compactness. For infinite‑dimensional \mathcal{H}, we can either work with the strong operator topology and require that the set be contained in a norm‑bounded ball that is compact in a weaker topology if needed; however, the existence of a maximum of a continuous function on a non‑compact set is not guaranteed. Since the rest of the monograph is finite‑dimensional in spirit (the value manifold is finite‑dimensional), we assume a finite‑dimensional approximation of \mathcal{H} for the purpose of the game, which is consistent with the use of empirical measures. With that, \mathcal{A}_{\text{amp}} is compact.
· The function a_i(A) = \frac{\|P_h A h_i\|}{\|P_h h_i\|} - 1 is continuous in A under the operator norm because A \mapsto \|P_h A h_i\| is continuous.
· The AI‑induced measure \rho_A is defined as the push‑forward of the empirical measure under the map v_i \mapsto \text{proj}_\mathcal{V}( A \iota(v_i) ), where \iota is the embedding of \mathcal{V} into \mathcal{H} and \text{proj} is a smooth closest‑point projection onto \mathcal{V} (well‑defined in a tubular neighborhood). This composition is smooth in A and the points, so \rho_A is a continuous function of A in the topology of weak convergence of measures. The KL divergence S_{KL}(\rho_A \| \rho_0) is lower semicontinuous in \rho_A under weak convergence; combined with continuity, it is continuous on the compact set, hence attains its maximum. In fact, because \rho_0 is fixed and \mathcal{V} is compact, the KL divergence is finite and continuous in the total variation metric under mild conditions (e.g., if \rho_0 has full support). We assume \rho_0 is smooth and bounded away from zero, ensuring continuity.

Thus all components of \Psi' are continuous on a compact domain, and a maximum exists. This justifies the equilibrium claim.

